verses.text:
Your oils have a pleasing scent;
your name is oil poured out.
That is why the young women love you.

On my bed, night after night,{a}
I sought the one my soul loves;
I sought him but did not find him.

Who is this coming up from the wilderness{b}
like columns of smoke,
perfumed with myrrh and frankincense,{c}
with all the powders of the merchant?

Look, it is Solomon’s litter!
Sixty warriors surround it,
from the warriors of Israel,

Look at you, my darling, so lovely,{a}
so lovely!
Doves are your eyes
behind your veil.
Your hair is a flock of goats
streaming down the slopes of Gilead.{b}

Come with me from Lebanon, my bride,{d}
come with me from Lebanon!
Descend from the peak of Amana,
from the summits of Senir and Hermon,{e}
from the dens of lions,
from the mountains of leopards.

You have made my heart race, my sister,{f} my bride;
you have made my heart race with one look from your eyes,
with one bead of your necklace.

Your lips drop honeycomb, my bride;
honey and milk lie beneath your tongue,
and your clothing smells like the cedars of Lebanon.

nard and saffron,{i}
calamus{j} and cinnamon,
with every tree of frankincense,
myrrh and aloes,{k}
with all the finest spices.

Wake up, north wind;
come, south wind!{l}
Blow on my garden,
let its spices flow out.
Let my beloved come into his garden
and eat its choicest fruits.

I have come into my garden, my sister, my bride;{a}
I have gathered my myrrh with my spice;
I have eaten my honeycomb with my honey;
I have drunk my wine with my milk.
Eat, friends, and drink;
drink deep, lovers!{b}

I slept, but my heart kept watch.{c}
Listen! My beloved knocks:
“Let me in, my sister, my darling,
my dove, my flawless one,
for my head is soaked with dew,
my curls with the night’s moisture.”

“I have removed my dress;
should I dress again?
I have washed my feet;
should I soil them?”

I opened to my beloved,
but my beloved had turned and gone.
My soul went out of me when he spoke.{e}
I searched for him but could not find him;
I called him, but he gave no answer.

The watchmen who go around the city found me;
they struck me, they bruised me;
the guards of the walls
took my shawl from me.

His head is finest gold;
his hair is in curls,{h}
black as a raven.

His arms are rods of gold
set with jewels of Tarshish.{k}
His body is a panel of ivory
covered with lapis lazuli.{l}

You are beautiful, my darling, as Tirzah,{c}
lovely as Jerusalem,
awe-inspiring as an army under banners.{d}

Turn around, turn around, O Shulammite!{h}
Turn around, turn around, and let us look at you!
Why would you look at the Shulammite
as at the dance of the two camps?{i}

How beautiful your feet in sandals,{a}
O noble daughter!{b}
The curves of your thighs are like jewelry,
the work of a master’s hands.

Your neck is like an ivory tower.
Your eyes are pools in Heshbon{c}
beside the gate of Bath-rabbim.{d}
Your nose is like the tower of Lebanon
that looks out toward Damascus.

Your head sits on you like Carmel,{e}
your loose hair gleams like purple;{f}
a king is caught in their tresses.

and your mouth like the best wine
flowing smoothly for my beloved,{h}
gliding over the lips of sleepers.{i}

Come, my beloved,
let us go out to the countryside;
let us spend the night among the henna bushes.{j}

I put you under oath, daughters of Jerusalem:
why rouse, why wake love
until it wishes?{c}

Who is this coming up from the wilderness,{d}
leaning on her beloved?
Under the apple tree I roused you;{e}
there your mother went into labor with you,
there she who bore you was in labor.

Set me as a seal over your heart,
as a seal on your arm,
for love is as fierce as death,
its passion{f} as unyielding as Sheol.
Its flashes are flashes of fire,
a flame of Yah.{g}

I was a wall,{i}
and my breasts were like towers.
So in his eyes I became
like one who finds peace.{j}

Solomon had a vineyard at Baal-hamon;{k}
he let the vineyard out to keepers.
For its fruit each one owed him
twenty-five pounds of silver.{l}

You who dwell in the gardens,{n}
friends are attentive to your voice;
let me be the one to hear it!
